EXT. ABANDONED SALT WORKS - EVAPORATION FLATS - LATE AFTERNOON

A white country, flat to the edge of the sky. Shallow ponds, each a different shade of rose and bone, are cut by crumbling wooden sluice gates. The wind carries a taste of brine. At the far end, a long shed with a sagging tin roof, its door hanging off one hinge.

TOBIAS WREN, 60s, kneels at a pond's edge, scooping salt crystals into a sack. Weathered as a fence post, his hat brim stiff with old sweat, his beard a pale yellow like old piano keys. His hands are cracked from decades of brine. He works without hurry.

A figure approaches along the causeway. ESPERANZA QUINTERO, 30s, rides a grey mule, a rifle slung across her back and a canvas satchel on her hip. Dust-coloured coat. Black braid. She stops the mule a polite distance away.

ESPERANZA
You Wren?

TOBIAS
Depends who's asking.

ESPERANZA
Esperanza Quintero. I bought the claim at the other end of the flats.

TOBIAS
That's a lot of nothing to buy.

ESPERANZA
It's cheap nothing.

He almost smiles. Drops another handful of salt into the sack.

TOBIAS
Water's bad on your side.

ESPERANZA
I figured.

TOBIAS
Good on mine. If you ask nicely.

She slides down from the mule. Boots crunch on the crust. She looks across the flats, then at him.

ESPERANZA
I'm asking.

A boy emerges from the shed, barefoot, nine or ten, LUKA, thin as a rope and just as strong. He carries a bucket almost as big as his torso.

LUKA
Grandpa, the pump's stuck again.

TOBIAS
Then unstick it.

LUKA
I tried.

TOBIAS
Try harder.

Luka notices Esperanza. Stares at the rifle. Stares at the mule.

LUKA
Is that a real gun?

ESPERANZA
It's a real mule.

LUKA
(grinning)
Can I touch him?

ESPERANZA
Her. Be gentle.

He sets down the bucket and approaches the mule with both palms out, whispering. The mule allows it. Tobias watches the boy, then the woman, then the horizon.

TOBIAS
Sit. There's coffee. It's terrible.

ESPERANZA
I've had worse.

TOBIAS
Not out here.

She smiles, ties the mule to a post, and follows him toward the shed. Behind them the salt shines like a field of broken glass under the long low sun.
